WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.190
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:06.810
Thank you so much for being here today.
&gt;&gt; Thank you for

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:07.883
including me.
&gt;&gt; I'm so

00:00:07.883 --> 00:00:12.600
excited to dig into that career and
your thoughts on the Valley.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.711
And I'm going to start us off with one of
the things that jumped out at me in my

00:00:15.711 --> 00:00:16.840
prep for this interview.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:22.780
You are an extremely generous tipper.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:23.908
&gt;&gt; I love that and

00:00:23.908 --> 00:00:28.873
I would like to know why.
&gt;&gt; So, I mean,

00:00:28.873 --> 00:00:34.972
it's well known and part of why I'm so
open about this is that I think

00:00:34.972 --> 00:00:40.017
it gives permission for
other people just to be honest.

00:00:40.017 --> 00:00:44.790
I grew up in a very kind of
dysfunctional household on welfare.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:50.280
And that compounded a bunch of shit
in my life that was not great.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.220
We were very focused on money.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:57.730
It was a huge point of pressure and
tension in the family.

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:01.150
It created massive depression
in my father, drinking.

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:03.490
Just, it was very dysfunctional.

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.959
And there was some point along
the way where I was like, okay,

00:01:06.959 --> 00:01:10.672
is money like really important or
not important?

00:01:10.672 --> 00:01:15.470
And I feel very lucky because I
don't think if you ask my sisters,

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:18.186
they got to the same place that I did.

00:01:18.186 --> 00:01:20.470
But I ended up not coveting it.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.530
And I found it to be something
that I could use to really

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:29.401
empower myself to do the things
that I wanted to do.

00:01:29.401 --> 00:01:32.120
And so, in a situation where you
know you go to like a restaurant.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.760
If you really empathize with
the people that are working there,

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:41.680
I see people who are like me,
brown skinned,

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.450
working hard,
creating these beautiful experiences.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:51.830
And then I can celebrate it by,
I guess, giving a YELP review.

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:55.095
But you can't buy food for
your kids with a fucking YELP review.

00:01:55.095 --> 00:01:56.467
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:56.467 --> 00:02:00.684
&gt;&gt; So I want a tip.

00:02:00.684 --> 00:02:03.032
And, I mean, it gives me so

00:02:03.032 --> 00:02:09.090
much joy because it's like
you'll have a 3 or $400 bill.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:13.620
In some cases, you tip 500 bucks or
1,000, and you close it.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.197
And they're expecting $40.

00:02:16.197 --> 00:02:20.050
And, I mean,
I'll get a little emotional, but

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:24.910
they will come out to you,
and it's transformational.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:26.990
And so it's so great.

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:30.207
It's just like little things
like that mean a lot of people.

00:02:30.207 --> 00:02:32.787
And just to be anonymously
generous like that,

00:02:32.787 --> 00:02:35.760
I think it's a great gift that
I have the ability to do.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:37.863
That's why I do it, I love it.
&gt;&gt; And

00:02:37.863 --> 00:02:40.817
it sounds like the experience
of being seen.

00:02:40.817 --> 00:02:43.229
And you are seen and
the individual is seen, and

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:45.856
you're sharing in that [INAUDIBLE].
&gt;&gt; Well I mean, it's funny.

00:02:45.856 --> 00:02:48.986
It's like 99% of the time
they don't say anything,

00:02:48.986 --> 00:02:52.230
because they do the bill thing afterwards.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.186
And they for sure can't pronounce my name.

00:02:54.186 --> 00:02:57.527
And so they for sure have no fucking
clue who it was that gave them the tip.

00:02:57.527 --> 00:02:59.980
It's fine, it's totally fine.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:02.770
But I love it, I absolutely love it.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:04.330
My friends freak out.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:05.570
Now it's caused tension actually,

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:09.630
because now when I go out with my friends,
they know as well.

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:13.474
And I've just made it very easy,
which is like, hey, if we're going out,

00:03:13.474 --> 00:03:14.748
let me just fucking pay.

00:03:14.748 --> 00:03:18.768
Just like, it's fine,
and it makes it simpler.

00:03:18.768 --> 00:03:19.268
Okay, yeah.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.928
I want to dig into what you were
describing about your upbringing and

00:03:23.928 --> 00:03:28.497
this unique position that you've gotten to
where you don't feel that you covet money.

00:03:28.497 --> 00:03:30.001
But you see it's power and

00:03:30.001 --> 00:03:33.900
the use that it has in furthering
some opportunities for you.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:36.580
How did you develop that?

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:39.080
How do you distinguish between luck and
skill?

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:42.941
How do you end up in that position?
&gt;&gt; So I,

00:03:46.209 --> 00:03:50.369
My parents craved money,
meaning they needed it

00:03:50.369 --> 00:03:55.820
because my dad was unemployed for
long stretches of time.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:57.710
My mom was the sole breadwinner.

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:02.280
She was a housekeeper,
then she was a nurse's aide.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:05.360
And I would just see how she grinded.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:06.970
We didn't have a car for a long time.

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:09.833
She takes the bus, we all take the bus.

00:04:09.833 --> 00:04:12.100
When I got my first job,
it was at Burger King.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:16.290
I'd take the money, and I had give it to
my parents, and we would buy bus passes.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:20.990
And I remember telling some of my friends,
I went to a very good high school,

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:23.280
kind of like the rich high school.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.686
Not the high school I should've gone to.

00:04:26.686 --> 00:04:29.220
I was able to go to this
different high school.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:34.090
And I would tell them, like, I would be so
ashamed that I worked at Burger King.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:37.941
They would sometimes come by,
and I would just be like fuck.

00:04:37.941 --> 00:04:42.713
And then at some point,
it was this release moment where I was,

00:04:42.713 --> 00:04:43.983
this is my life.

00:04:43.983 --> 00:04:45.940
I can't do anything about it.

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:47.858
This is what it is right now.

00:04:47.858 --> 00:04:51.650
And I kind of had a sense that I could
figure some stuff out later, but

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:53.150
I didn't really know.

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:54.690
And so I just accepted it.

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:57.480
And then the minute I accepted it,
I wasn't ashamed anymore.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:01.353
And then, when I wasn't ashamed I could
start to actually be inside my head, like,

00:05:01.353 --> 00:05:02.650
what really matters?

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:06.250
So then what happened was there was these
massive racial riots in Los Angeles.

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:09.320
And not as if the fucking American
government did anything about it.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:12.360
But the Canadian government was like,
shit, let's get all these black and

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:17.260
brown kids jobs, because we don't want
the Rodney King riots in Toronto, and

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:19.060
Ottawa, and parts of Ontario.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:24.128
And so I was able to take all of my dad's
rejection letters, call every single one.

00:05:24.128 --> 00:05:26.020
And one of them gave me a job.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:29.050
And I worked at the well known
telecommunication start up in Ottawa.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:31.960
Ottawa had a really burgeoning
tech sense at the time.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:35.510
And I worked in this organization that
was run by this really iconic guy,

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:36.880
Terry Matthews.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:37.780
And he was a billionaire.

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:39.981
And I was like,
what the fuck is that word?

00:05:39.981 --> 00:05:43.700
I mean,
I didn't even know a fucking thousandaire.

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:45.868
I was like what is a billionaire?

00:05:45.868 --> 00:05:50.700
And this guy was risk on.

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:58.900
And he was just so dynamic in
the businesses he had started and

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:02.970
how he viewed his place in the world,
and I was enthralled.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:05.180
I was 9 degrees away from him.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:09.590
But the cult of personality around him in
that business, the lore, when it trickles

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:16.625
down to you, was not about his
conspicuous consumption or anything else.

00:06:16.625 --> 00:06:20.155
It was about okay,
we're launching this frame relay switch.

00:06:20.155 --> 00:06:21.695
Now we're going to buy this company.

00:06:21.695 --> 00:06:23.775
Okay, we're going to pivot
the entire business to ATM.

00:06:23.775 --> 00:06:27.285
And you were just, it was an amazing time.

00:06:28.425 --> 00:06:33.990
And so I took the bus to Newbridge.

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:40.060
Complete luck that the controller
of Newbridge would

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:44.870
go from his nice neighborhood through this
shitty neighborhood to get on the highway.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:47.441
And so he would see this
guy standing by the bus and

00:06:47.441 --> 00:06:49.592
eventually he saw me inside the office.

00:06:49.592 --> 00:06:51.430
And one day he stopped.

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:52.330
He said, you want a ride?

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:53.023
Do you work at Newbridge?

00:06:53.023 --> 00:06:54.150
I'm like, yeah.

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:55.960
This guy, Sam Legg.

00:06:55.960 --> 00:07:02.686
So in the car, he was in the front seat,
like beside Terry.

00:07:02.686 --> 00:07:05.785
And he was able to tell me how
this guy thought about this.

00:07:05.785 --> 00:07:09.689
because I would read it in the paper,
and I'd be like, why is he doing this?

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.950
So it just completely
rewired what money was.

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:17.530
For Terry Matthews,
money was an instrument of change, right?

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:21.410
He had a market cap, he's like, wow,
that's 8 billion dollars of change,

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:25.090
10 billion dollars of change,
15 billion dollars of change.

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:27.480
Versus, there was another guy
building a company at the time.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:31.510
His name was Michael Copeland,
who ran a company called Corelle,

00:07:31.510 --> 00:07:33.250
also a billionaire.

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:39.450
And he was the exact opposite, had a messy
divorce, married some trophy wife.

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:46.177
He took this obscene shiny glass from his
building and covered his house in it.

00:07:46.177 --> 00:07:50.450
And so you have this dichotomy of two
different characters at that time in

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:53.680
the 90sm building really
interesting businesses.

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:58.160
Correll, which was a regional business,
Newbridge, which was regional business.

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:02.530
They were both very successful, but they
manifested money so totally differently.

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:04.140
And I was like, I want to be this guy.

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:07.930
I want to be this mega-compounder,

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:11.460
swashbuckling around, trying to
do really cool shit in the world.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:12.740
How do I do that?

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:17.660
And Sam helped me because you could
understand from him what Terry cared about

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:19.990
and then, for me, I just copied.

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:23.670
I mean a lot of my life, quite honestly,
is just copying the things that I see.

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:27.280
There's not a lot of original
thought here, there truly is not.

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:31.715
We can all pretend we're all fucking
geniuses, honestly, be good copiers.

00:08:31.715 --> 00:08:34.570
Do you know what I'm saying?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:35.837
&gt;&gt; It's the best thing in the world.

00:08:35.837 --> 00:08:39.666
Be around high functioning,
high quality people and

00:08:39.666 --> 00:08:43.670
just copy the shit that they do.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:08:43.670 --> 00:08:46.690
&gt;&gt; Observe the shit that's kind of crappy

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:48.410
and then don't do that stuff.

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:53.070
It's not a fucking complicated formula.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:55.050
&gt;&gt; When did you use that best?

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:57.762
&gt;&gt; No, all the time, now it's funny,

00:08:57.762 --> 00:09:03.760
it's like, you have a surface area of
ways to spend your time now, or I do.

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:08.626
And I'm in this midst now where we
are really scaling up what we're doing as

00:09:08.626 --> 00:09:09.483
a business.

00:09:09.483 --> 00:09:14.400
And I meet these people that to me are so
iconic that they were like

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:18.589
arms length people that are now
sitting face to face and

00:09:18.589 --> 00:09:24.470
they treat me like a quasi-peer,
I wouldn't say peer, but quasi-peer.

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:29.890
But what I see is,
I see some amazing stuff and I see some

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:34.290
utter dysfunction in some of the richest,
most important people in the world.

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:37.520
And I have to decide
how am I going to act?

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:38.720
What am I going to embody?

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:39.600
What am I copying?

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:41.260
What am I not copying?

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:50.340
And so I get a chance to now refine
my decision-making and discipline.

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:52.341
And it's, so that's how it plays out.

00:09:52.341 --> 00:09:55.985
I don't know if I answered your question.
&gt;&gt; I'm thinking very,

00:09:55.985 --> 00:09:59.133
there's a deep connection between
money as an instrument of change and

00:09:59.133 --> 00:10:01.355
what you're doing now at Social Capital.
&gt;&gt; Look,

00:10:01.355 --> 00:10:05.170
here's the thing, there's about
150 people that run the world.

00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:09.895
Anybody who wants to go into politics,
they're all fucking puppets, okay?

00:10:09.895 --> 00:10:11.460
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:12.380
&gt;&gt; There are 150,

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:17.900
and they're all men that run the world,
period, full stop.

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:21.960
They control most of the important assets,
they control the money flows.

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:24.020
And these are not the tech entrepeneurs.

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:28.720
Now they are going to get rolled
over over the next five to ten years

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:32.130
by the people that are really
underneath pulling the strings.

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:35.980
And when you get behind the curtain and
see how that world works,

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:40.270
what you realize is, it is unfairly
set up for them and their progeny.

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:45.960
Now, I'm not going to say that that's
something that we can rip apart.

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:50.600
But first order of business is, I want to
break through and be at that table.

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:52.450
That's the first order of business.

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:56.690
And the way that I do that is by
proving that I can do what they do

00:10:56.690 --> 00:11:01.270
as well as they do it and
then do it better than how they do it.

00:11:01.270 --> 00:11:05.490
Because at the end of the day,
they are commercial fucking animals, okay?

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:07.496
And they'll open the door
out of curiosity and

00:11:07.496 --> 00:11:09.301
they'll let me stay because I add value.

00:11:09.301 --> 00:11:11.758
And then once I'm there,
I can open the door for

00:11:11.758 --> 00:11:14.590
other people who can try
to do the same thing.

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:19.780
So my entire goal now is that,
is to be in a position to aggregate enough

00:11:19.780 --> 00:11:25.280
of the capital of the world, to then
reallocate it against my world view.

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:29.100
And I'm not saying my world view is
the best or right, but it is mine.

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:32.875
And at the end of the day, there are 150
other fucking guys with their world view

00:11:32.875 --> 00:11:36.280
and they don't give a shit what you
think about their fucking world view.

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:38.390
That's the truth.

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:40.960
And so, why not me?

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:44.090
Why not?

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:45.200
Why not one of you?

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:46.720
Why not?

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:49.730
And so in my life now I'm
just kind of like, why not?

00:11:49.730 --> 00:11:55.358
Why the fuck not?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] [APPLAUSE]

00:11:55.358 --> 00:11:56.835
&gt;&gt; Fuck it!

00:11:56.835 --> 00:12:01.249
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; So you have these 50 year goals for

00:12:01.249 --> 00:12:03.902
Social Capital, three of them.

00:12:03.902 --> 00:12:08.581
Can you give us your world
view through those goals?

00:12:09.830 --> 00:12:11.769
&gt;&gt; So my 2045 ambition for

00:12:11.769 --> 00:12:16.080
the organization,
this is as I see it right now, 2045.

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:20.107
Is I would like us to employ at
least ten million people through

00:12:20.107 --> 00:12:21.970
the businesses that we own.

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:26.258
I would like our businesses
to positively affect at least

00:12:26.258 --> 00:12:29.295
a quarter of the world's population and

00:12:29.295 --> 00:12:34.970
I would like us to have made
cumulatively about a trillion dollars.

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:35.470
That's my 2045 ambition.

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:42.359
I think if we do that, we are the modern
face of capitalism for the next 50 years.

00:12:43.570 --> 00:12:47.190
So, how we do that now
becomes really important.

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:52.350
So if you think about building businesses
today that could do any of those three

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:57.442
things, you have to be technological,
you absolutely just have to be.

00:12:57.442 --> 00:12:59.506
You are not going to build a brick and

00:12:59.506 --> 00:13:03.780
mortar business that gets anywhere close
to those three things, number one.

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:07.380
Number two is,
you have to think very broadly

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:10.550
about building things that
can stand the test of time.

00:13:10.550 --> 00:13:14.360
Those businesses are by definition
hard and non-obvious and so

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:17.410
they compound in scale very slowly.

00:13:17.410 --> 00:13:21.830
And that's really counterintuitive to
the overriding logic of Silicon Valley,

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:24.930
which says, quick, fast, go, right?

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:28.390
But that kind of premature ejaculation
doesn't work if you're trying to fucking

00:13:28.390 --> 00:13:29.790
be around forever.

00:13:29.790 --> 00:13:35.570
You gotta be in the flow for
decades, that is hard.

00:13:35.570 --> 00:13:39.120
It tests people's patience,
it tests people's resolve,

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:42.250
it tests people's really
intellectual incision.

00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:45.860
And this is where everybody gives up,
everybody gives up.

00:13:45.860 --> 00:13:51.762
I had this moment today, just a total
different way of describing this.

00:13:51.762 --> 00:13:54.681
Through the Warriors,
I've met a lot of players obviously,

00:13:54.681 --> 00:13:58.570
one of them who I had become
unbelievably close to is David Lee.

00:13:58.570 --> 00:14:02.929
David Lee just got engaged to this
unbelievable woman, Caroline Wozniacki,

00:14:02.929 --> 00:14:03.860
tennis player.

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:07.110
We were working out this morning, okay?

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:09.935
Couples work out, me and
my wife, him and his fiancee.

00:14:09.935 --> 00:14:10.840
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:11.890
&gt;&gt; So we're doing this thing,

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:15.110
this treadmill thing,
which my trainer concocted.

00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:17.739
And I'm in pretty good shape.

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:25.350
And then you see what it really means to
be extremely dedicated, in their case,

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:28.600
to something which is physical fitness
because they're professional athletes.

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:33.280
When I give up,
when the average human gives up,

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:36.940
their brain goes into fucking overdrive,
okay?

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:40.050
And they're like,
there's this next level burst.

00:14:40.050 --> 00:14:42.818
And so it's that.

00:14:42.818 --> 00:14:45.750
It's like when things get
hard can you double down?

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:47.850
A different example, we had yesterday,

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:51.840
we do these things every quarter called
quarterly portfolio reviews where

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:55.860
incredibly intensive review of our entire
portfolio, revisiting our strategy,

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:59.100
talking about all the things we're doing
to make sure we're on the right track.

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:03.100
And I had this observation which was,
I started a diabetes business.

00:15:03.100 --> 00:15:05.150
My dad died of diabetes three years ago.

00:15:05.150 --> 00:15:06.690
Most of my relatives have it.

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:10.820
I express the gene that makes me
disproportionately susceptible to it.

00:15:10.820 --> 00:15:12.800
So it's something that I
personally care about.

00:15:13.990 --> 00:15:16.610
But that business took seven years

00:15:17.810 --> 00:15:21.590
to get to a place where we even
have a chance of being viable.

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:28.071
And I think to myself, in that example,
I embodied a bit of Caroline Wozniacki.

00:15:28.071 --> 00:15:31.860
When things got hard,
I pushed through the pain.

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:36.640
Now in my case, the pain was the fear of
failure, continuing to write more money,

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:39.520
a belief that I had that this
business was going to work.

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:43.200
And today,
that business manages a million diabetics.

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:47.102
And I think there is some kid out there
whose family will be slightly less

00:15:47.102 --> 00:15:50.890
dysfunctional because their
parent's disease is better managed.

00:15:50.890 --> 00:15:55.340
It's not going to go to a place where
like, they're getting dyalized, or

00:15:55.340 --> 00:15:57.260
they're on an organ registry.

00:15:58.370 --> 00:16:00.770
And that value for
that kid in our world and

00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:05.050
all of you that benefits will never be
connected the dots, but that’s okay.

00:16:05.050 --> 00:16:10.529
But it required, just,
resilience and I'm proud of that.

00:16:10.529 --> 00:16:16.077
And so I want to bring back a little bit
of that here so that it's not, quick,

00:16:16.077 --> 00:16:23.050
fast, five million users, ten million now,
flip it, sell it, great celebrate.

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:25.650
It's like,
hey let's work on some hard shit together.

00:16:25.650 --> 00:16:27.620
Let's buckle down,
look each other in the eye,

00:16:27.620 --> 00:16:29.460
and say we're going to
support each other here.

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:32.440
This is going to take seven or
eight years, but if it works,

00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:33.900
it will really mean something.

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:37.020
It's something that matters to you for

00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:41.060
something that's other than just simple
motivation of the social capital you get

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:44.660
from your friends or
money in the short term.

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:46.140
And winning bigger longer term.

00:16:47.690 --> 00:16:48.770
We're missing that and

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:52.340
I think part of our success will
be if we can stay focused on that.

00:16:52.340 --> 00:16:54.670
Our education portfolio is another thing.

00:16:54.670 --> 00:16:57.510
It literally looked like a train wreck for

00:16:57.510 --> 00:17:01.990
three years, where everybody has always
said you never invest in education.

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:04.160
And my thing was,
it's not about education.

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:05.990
This is about human capital development.

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:09.770
It's about, for every one of you who is so
smart, that got into Stanford,

00:17:09.770 --> 00:17:12.380
think of all the people
who could be as capable.

00:17:12.380 --> 00:17:17.402
I'm not asking you to empathize with them,
but I, as a capitalist, want a starting

00:17:17.402 --> 00:17:21.747
line where all of those people can run
a race so that I can pick the winner.

00:17:21.747 --> 00:17:25.953
No offense to any of you here.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:17:25.953 --> 00:17:27.540
&gt;&gt; And it took us three years we're now at

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:29.530
those building blocks exist and
I'm like wow.

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:33.440
How many times did I have to
look at my partners and say no?

00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:36.430
The easy answer is to walk away,
the hard answer is to double down,

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:38.440
we have to support this business.

00:17:38.440 --> 00:17:40.590
That culture's missing here.

00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:44.710
So part of what we're trying to do to
achieve those goals is take really

00:17:44.710 --> 00:17:49.750
big audacious points of view on the world
and then train ourselves to be patient.

00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:51.570
And it's really, really hard.

00:17:51.570 --> 00:17:55.520
The entire society is set up
to not be patient anymore.

00:17:55.520 --> 00:17:58.635
&gt;&gt; And I'm hearing also a conflict with

00:17:58.635 --> 00:18:04.364
this fail fast and learn mentality
where if you're taking a deep and

00:18:04.364 --> 00:18:08.197
big bet,
like you want it to be the right one.

00:18:08.197 --> 00:18:12.563
How do you know when that's true?
&gt;&gt; I think that that stale fast approach

00:18:12.563 --> 00:18:15.937
works in consumer Internet businesses,
but I don't think it works for

00:18:15.937 --> 00:18:17.472
anything that really matters.

00:18:17.472 --> 00:18:22.106
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Basically.

00:18:22.106 --> 00:18:24.330
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:18:24.330 --> 00:18:26.290
&gt;&gt; Consumer Internet businesses are about

00:18:26.290 --> 00:18:31.740
exploiting psychology, and that is one
where you want to fail fast because

00:18:31.740 --> 00:18:34.810
you know people aren't predictable and so
we want to psychologically figure out how

00:18:34.810 --> 00:18:39.360
to manipulate you as fast as possible and
then give you back that dopamine hit.

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:42.715
We did that brilliantly at Facebook,
Instagram has done it,

00:18:42.715 --> 00:18:46.690
WhatsApp has done it,
Snapchat has done it, Twitter has done it.

00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:47.790
So there are great examples.

00:18:47.790 --> 00:18:49.140
WeChat is doing it.

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:52.040
There are great examples of
failing fast is the right

00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:55.960
path to exploiting psychology
of mass populations of people.

00:18:55.960 --> 00:18:57.710
But it is not how you solve diabetes.

00:18:57.710 --> 00:19:00.150
It is not how you use precision
medicine to cure cancer.

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:03.320
It is not how you educate broad
swats of the world's population.

00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:04.920
It's just not.

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:07.600
It's taking a point of view and
being methodical.

00:19:09.970 --> 00:19:13.950
It's hard, no it's hard.
&gt;&gt; I'm glad that you're

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:16.720
taking a stab at it.
&gt;&gt; By the way, if it works,

00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:17.840
it's where all the money comes.

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:23.200
If you talk about where all of the gains,
where all of

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:27.720
the value will be created over the next
50 years, it'll be in those hard things.

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:31.367
And the reason is because those
white spaces are so wide open.

00:19:31.367 --> 00:19:33.928
The competitive pressures
are non-existent.

