the more rational actors we have and
the more of a balanced,

00:28:50.890 --> 00:28:54.520
fair system,
that is what diversity really means to me.

00:28:56.140 --> 00:29:01.280
It doesn't mean let's manage back
to a quota, it doesn't mean,

00:29:01.280 --> 00:29:05.660
okay, we need a black guy, a brown guy,
we need a few, that's not what it means.

00:29:05.660 --> 00:29:08.600
This is what it means, right?

00:29:08.600 --> 00:29:12.990
There are people in this audience
that have probably dealt with

00:29:12.990 --> 00:29:15.990
the same kind of like life
that I've dealt with.

00:29:15.990 --> 00:29:19.200
You have a very unique
worldview that matters.

00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:24.213
In the absence of capital,
you're irrelevant, with capital,

00:29:24.213 --> 00:29:27.820
you're powerful.

00:29:27.820 --> 00:29:31.950
And then you decide in small ways,
in medium size ways,

00:29:31.950 --> 00:29:37.680
in large ways, right, and it just depends
on the scale of capital that you have.

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:40.820
And so
that's what's necessary now in capitalism,

00:29:40.820 --> 00:29:44.380
which is that we have to come
back to what is it really?

00:29:44.380 --> 00:29:46.390
Is it an economic system, yes.

00:29:46.390 --> 00:29:49.780
But it is a philosophy as well, right?

00:29:49.780 --> 00:29:53.060
And it's a philosophy that
says we will vote for

00:29:53.060 --> 00:29:56.960
the change we intend based
on the views that we have.

00:29:56.960 --> 00:30:02.657
And when you look at the most successful
people that operate in these tentacles,

00:30:02.657 --> 00:30:04.260
that's what they do.

00:30:04.260 --> 00:30:06.060
Look at the Koch brothers,
that's what they do.

00:30:06.060 --> 00:30:10.590
They have built a network of
influence based on capital.

00:30:11.770 --> 00:30:17.160
Their world view is propagated into the
world at an unbelievably aggressive rate

00:30:17.160 --> 00:30:20.820
that has been compounding for
decades, that is genius.

00:30:22.607 --> 00:30:27.110
It is genius, it doesn't matter whether
you agree with their world view.

00:30:27.110 --> 00:30:29.910
The question is, what's
the counterweight to their world view?

00:30:31.560 --> 00:30:32.770
Can you name one?

00:30:33.810 --> 00:30:38.370
Can you name a group of people that
operate in the exact same way,

00:30:38.370 --> 00:30:42.910
methodically, meticulously
assembling capital, influence,

00:30:42.910 --> 00:30:45.135
assets who believe the opposite?

00:30:45.135 --> 00:30:48.740
Irrespective of what
you think of the Kochs.

00:30:48.740 --> 00:30:52.850
You want both views out
in the world at scale.

00:30:52.850 --> 00:30:54.699
Well right now, you have this and
you don't have this.

00:30:56.870 --> 00:30:58.770
And there are many examples of that.

00:30:58.770 --> 00:31:02.180
And so get the money and

00:31:02.180 --> 00:31:06.060
don't lose your moral compass when you do.
&gt;&gt; You've

00:31:07.300 --> 00:31:11.460
said that you think VC is
a horrendous allocator of capital.

00:31:11.460 --> 00:31:16.528
So if we need to go get the capital,
how do we do it better?

00:31:16.528 --> 00:31:22.138
Well, we are chipping away,
I think other people will copy us.

00:31:22.138 --> 00:31:26.348
Venture is basically just a bunch of

00:31:26.348 --> 00:31:29.453
soulless cowards.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:31:29.453 --> 00:31:33.449
&gt;&gt; They're well intentioned, but

00:31:33.449 --> 00:31:40.827
they're well intentioned soulless cowards.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:31:40.827 --> 00:31:44.346
&gt;&gt; They have fallen for what everybody

00:31:44.346 --> 00:31:49.280
else falls for,
which you can't blame them for.

00:31:49.280 --> 00:31:53.254
Which is here is this gold star and
they're like, wow,

00:31:53.254 --> 00:31:55.122
I've gotten a gold star.

00:31:55.122 --> 00:31:58.254
And then the minute you do that what you
do is you start to just choke it really

00:31:58.254 --> 00:32:00.710
tight and you're like I don't
want to lose this gold star.

00:32:00.710 --> 00:32:03.020
And then what happens?

00:32:03.020 --> 00:32:03.770
You go risk off.

00:32:05.090 --> 00:32:06.110
And then what happens?

00:32:06.110 --> 00:32:09.470
Your behaviors are about protecting
the seat of power that you have,

00:32:09.470 --> 00:32:13.060
the social definition that
that seat affords you.

00:32:13.060 --> 00:32:17.180
And so now instead of being an accelerant
of a progressive worldview,

00:32:17.180 --> 00:32:20.420
you become a retardant to
a progressive worldview, right?

00:32:21.490 --> 00:32:22.960
Your risk appetite shrinks,

00:32:22.960 --> 00:32:26.385
the surface area in which you're
willing to make decisions shrink.

00:32:26.385 --> 00:32:28.942
And when you are a critical
part of the future, again,

00:32:28.942 --> 00:32:32.554
going back to how I started, the most
important part of capitalism is going to

00:32:32.554 --> 00:32:35.880
be the folks that are driving
the technological change.

00:32:35.880 --> 00:32:39.022
Well, as a class,
that's what VCs are supposed to do.

00:32:39.022 --> 00:32:41.988
But they're by and large,
in my opinion, the wrong people.

00:32:41.988 --> 00:32:44.220
So how do we do it?

00:32:44.220 --> 00:32:47.674
You have to make it more systematic,
you have to make it more data driven,

00:32:47.674 --> 00:32:50.535
you have to strip out the bias,
you have to be open 24/7.

00:32:50.535 --> 00:32:53.203
You can't have these dumb rules of like,

00:32:53.203 --> 00:32:57.330
I only do deals if they're within
15 miles driving distance.

00:32:58.390 --> 00:33:03.928
How can the most important part of,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:33:03.928 --> 00:33:05.541
&gt;&gt; I mean, can you imagine if I walked

00:33:05.541 --> 00:33:09.342
into Zuck's office, Facebook, where
I'm like, I have a great fucking idea.

00:33:09.342 --> 00:33:12.778
Facebook is only going to be open for
business Monday through Friday.

00:33:12.778 --> 00:33:16.055
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:33:16.055 --> 00:33:17.760
&gt;&gt; 10 to 5, because we want to get home.

00:33:18.810 --> 00:33:22.574
Summers we're going to take off, and
so but just going to show a 404 page.

00:33:22.574 --> 00:33:24.490
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:33:24.490 --> 00:33:25.686
&gt;&gt; Does it make sense?

00:33:25.686 --> 00:33:28.522
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:33:28.522 --> 00:33:29.219
&gt;&gt; Or Google,

00:33:29.219 --> 00:33:32.161
we only want you to search
during business hours.

00:33:32.161 --> 00:33:34.970
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:33:34.970 --> 00:33:38.560
&gt;&gt; And so you have to be open for scale,

00:33:38.560 --> 00:33:42.060
you have to be global, you have to do
all of these things so that you can get

00:33:42.060 --> 00:33:47.070
the money into the hands of the people
that deserve it and then help them.

00:33:47.070 --> 00:33:51.630
And by helping them, what you have to do
is actually address the core structural

00:33:51.630 --> 00:33:54.610
parts of business-building
that are not done well today.

00:33:54.610 --> 00:33:56.349
And the best analog is AWS.

00:33:57.500 --> 00:34:01.750
If you were starting a business 15 years
ago, I can even tell you a decade ago,

00:34:01.750 --> 00:34:07.310
we were racking servers at our data
center, because the growth was great.

00:34:09.240 --> 00:34:12.100
And somewhere along the way,
Amazon said, you know what?

00:34:12.100 --> 00:34:14.970
All of that complexity is necessary, but

00:34:14.970 --> 00:34:18.380
it's not sufficient to building
Facebook or any other great company.

00:34:18.380 --> 00:34:20.440
So let me abstract that for you.

00:34:20.440 --> 00:34:25.610
Let me go and replace all of those
good people with an API, and

00:34:25.610 --> 00:34:28.620
I'll take those good people and
they'll be celebrated at Amazon.

00:34:31.070 --> 00:34:35.067
And initially a few people said yes, and
people thought in 2007 AWS was a joke.

00:34:35.067 --> 00:34:37.161
And we used to laugh at it.

00:34:37.161 --> 00:34:39.955
We were like,
why would anybody do this stuff?

00:34:39.955 --> 00:34:44.560
And now see how fast a decade later
you're on the other side of the fence.

00:34:44.560 --> 00:34:47.604
If any of you guys start a business and
you're not on AWS,

00:34:47.604 --> 00:34:50.120
you would be the joke, right?

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:51.680
So what did they do?

00:34:51.680 --> 00:34:55.530
They basically said, here's all this stuff
that's necessary to building a business.

00:34:57.290 --> 00:34:58.560
You need to do it.

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:00.140
You probably don't want to do it.

00:35:00.140 --> 00:35:02.296
You're probably not going to do it well.

00:35:02.296 --> 00:35:04.840
And so let me just replace
it all with an abstraction.

00:35:04.840 --> 00:35:09.170
Your company can be smaller, leaner, more
efficient, and now the money you raise

00:35:09.170 --> 00:35:13.270
can be used for things that
approximate core product value better.

00:35:13.270 --> 00:35:16.610
Okay, now in 2017, ask the same question.

00:35:16.610 --> 00:35:18.740
What's the next set of
things that make sense?

00:35:19.790 --> 00:35:23.620
And in my opinion, what we uncovered at
Facebook is what every company needs,

00:35:23.620 --> 00:35:28.800
which is how do you shift from
an anecdotal, political capital,

00:35:28.800 --> 00:35:32.380
social capital-oriented way of
making decisions inside a company

00:35:32.380 --> 00:35:35.880
to a very precise, unemotional,
data driven process?

00:35:37.360 --> 00:35:39.910
And the way you do it is by building
a bunch of data infrastructure,

00:35:39.910 --> 00:35:41.840
by collecting a bunch of data itself,

00:35:41.840 --> 00:35:46.360
by helping you optimize your business in
a bunch of obvious and non-obvious ways.

00:35:46.360 --> 00:35:50.250
And so what we believe is that we should
take that capability that my team

00:35:50.250 --> 00:35:54.600
built at Facebook and abstract it for
all of you who intend to start a company.

00:35:55.610 --> 00:35:59.080
So now in the future,
what should happen is, you'll go to AWS,

00:35:59.080 --> 00:36:02.080
you'll get some infrastructure,
you'll go to socialcapital.com, and

00:36:02.080 --> 00:36:04.220
you'll get some insights and knowledge.

00:36:04.220 --> 00:36:06.580
And it's all interacting via APIs.

00:36:06.580 --> 00:36:09.539
It's all interaction via endpoints and
primitives.

00:36:10.680 --> 00:36:14.870
And what happens is we can
now learn from the feedback,

00:36:14.870 --> 00:36:17.370
from your data, how you're doing.

00:36:17.370 --> 00:36:21.170
We can merge those learnings across many,
many other businesses.

00:36:21.170 --> 00:36:26.060
We can find things in some remote
part of some company over here and

00:36:26.060 --> 00:36:28.990
translate that as value for you over here.

00:36:28.990 --> 00:36:30.130
Wouldn't you want that?

00:36:30.130 --> 00:36:31.130
Of course you'd want that.

00:36:32.320 --> 00:36:34.970
What if we could do more
efficient customer acquisition?

00:36:34.970 --> 00:36:37.310
What if we could figure out churn better?

00:36:37.310 --> 00:36:40.940
So all of these things now are what
we are going to bring to the market,

00:36:40.940 --> 00:36:43.410
systematic capabilities.

00:36:43.410 --> 00:36:44.980
And I think over time what that does is,

00:36:44.980 --> 00:36:49.570
again, it moves to a much more progressive
view of what capitalism should be.

00:36:49.570 --> 00:36:54.800
It's not the few that matched
a human driven pattern, but

00:36:54.800 --> 00:36:59.730
it's more about taking big shots, scaling
up the money, being able to support people

00:36:59.730 --> 00:37:03.460
in structurally predictable ways, and
allowing them to build better companies.

00:37:03.460 --> 00:37:05.970
Those companies, I suspect,
will be as profitable.

00:37:05.970 --> 00:37:07.340
But they'll be smaller.

00:37:07.340 --> 00:37:08.710
They'll be more efficient.

00:37:08.710 --> 00:37:10.060
They'll have more revenue per head.

00:37:10.060 --> 00:37:14.440
All the things that you would want over
time so that instead of having four or

00:37:14.440 --> 00:37:17.020
five mega companies,
you could have millions and

00:37:17.020 --> 00:37:20.650
millions and millions of smaller,
better run companies.

00:37:20.650 --> 00:37:21.880
And that's my world view.

00:37:21.880 --> 00:37:23.000
And I think if we do that,

00:37:23.000 --> 00:37:27.950
we achieve our goals, all of that stuff
comes together if we enable that.

00:37:27.950 --> 00:37:30.050
So what you're seeing from us is that.

00:37:30.050 --> 00:37:33.780
And it's really upsetting and
disconcerting to the established

00:37:33.780 --> 00:37:38.760
sort of venture classes, because it
up-ends how they've defined their value.

00:37:38.760 --> 00:37:41.370
It's a very classic inventor's dilemma.

00:37:41.370 --> 00:37:46.400
They are like,
in my brain is pattern-recognition.

00:37:46.400 --> 00:37:50.350
But we know that that's not true, and
the data shows you that that's not true.

00:37:50.350 --> 00:37:54.180
Simple question for all of you guys
that love the allure of Silicon Valley.

00:37:54.180 --> 00:37:58.982
From the 70s when Microsoft was founded
to now, if you look at all the major

00:37:58.982 --> 00:38:03.260
companies that have been created,
major, say over 50 billion,

00:38:03.260 --> 00:38:08.089
over 60 billion, what percentage of
the Series A investors overlapped?

00:38:08.089 --> 00:38:12.953
You did the A of Microsoft, and you did
the A of Google or you did the A of Uber,

00:38:12.953 --> 00:38:14.180
right?

00:38:14.180 --> 00:38:18.596
Like for pattern recognition, again,
it's non-numerical, right, it's about wow,

00:38:18.596 --> 00:38:24.210
I like the cut of this guy's jib.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:38:24.210 --> 00:38:28.331
&gt;&gt; Well, the answer is it's less than 2%.

00:38:28.331 --> 00:38:29.610
So what does that mean?

00:38:29.610 --> 00:38:32.800
Everybody's bullshitting,
that's what it really means, right?

00:38:33.940 --> 00:38:39.476
If you're in a seat, and you have good
deal flow, and you have precious capital,

00:38:39.476 --> 00:38:43.489
and there's a massive tailwind
of technological change,

00:38:43.489 --> 00:38:48.320
over time you get one of the 20,
and you look like a genius.

00:38:48.320 --> 00:38:50.580
That's basically what's happened, and

00:38:50.580 --> 00:38:54.240
nobody wants to admit that,
but that's the fucking truth.

00:38:57.417 --> 00:38:58.910
It's the truth.

00:38:58.910 --> 00:39:00.080
So if anybody looks at you and

00:39:00.080 --> 00:39:01.910
tells you they know what the fuck
they're doing, they're lying.

00:39:03.510 --> 00:39:07.600
They're lying, they are lying
to themselves at a minimum, and

00:39:07.600 --> 00:39:09.030
at a maximum they're lying to you.

00:39:10.380 --> 00:39:14.300
Because the data does not support
that claim, and that's irrefutable.

00:39:16.046 --> 00:39:19.876
So I don't want to be one of those
people that stumble into a company and

00:39:19.876 --> 00:39:22.728
look back and like,
yeah, we were geniuses.

00:39:22.728 --> 00:39:23.830
I want us to be systematic.

00:39:25.260 --> 00:39:28.670
And I want us to be able to deploy tools
and capabilities that get us closer and

00:39:28.670 --> 00:39:31.020
closer to that.

00:39:31.020 --> 00:39:33.090
And that is what if you're again,

00:39:33.090 --> 00:39:37.220
a traditionalist, freaks you out.
&gt;&gt; And that's what allows you to make

00:39:37.220 --> 00:39:41.350
these much longer term commitments
to businesses that are going to

00:39:41.350 --> 00:39:45.085
address healthcare, education.
&gt;&gt; Well, no, that's just because I'm rich.

00:39:45.085 --> 00:39:46.100
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:39:46.100 --> 00:39:48.210
&gt;&gt; But just to be honest.

00:39:48.210 --> 00:39:50.129
I don't need anybody else's money.

00:39:50.129 --> 00:39:52.532
Look, this is not a popular view.

00:39:52.532 --> 00:39:57.092
So the first fucking billion
dollars is mine, and

00:39:57.092 --> 00:40:01.881
then I have to prove it and
then other people glom on.

00:40:01.881 --> 00:40:05.985
But, I mean Many of them
started by caring, but

00:40:05.985 --> 00:40:09.615
I suspect over time, if we end up
with a hundred, two hundred or

00:40:09.615 --> 00:40:14.296
five hundred billion dollars, how much
of that last 50 to 60% really care?

00:40:14.296 --> 00:40:19.051
That's okay, I want the fucking money.

00:40:19.051 --> 00:40:23.360
I will play the goddamned game and
I'll win, because I want the money.

00:40:24.770 --> 00:40:26.780
because I want to extend my influence,

00:40:26.780 --> 00:40:28.320
do you know what I'm saying?
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:40:28.320 --> 00:40:29.825
&gt;&gt; They want to return, it's fine.

00:40:29.825 --> 00:40:30.470
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:40:30.470 --> 00:40:32.195
&gt;&gt; Yeah, he wants the money.

00:40:32.195 --> 00:40:34.950
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:40:34.950 --> 00:40:37.070
&gt;&gt; because I want to win, I so

00:40:37.070 --> 00:40:41.111
deeply want to win.
&gt;&gt; How,

00:40:41.111 --> 00:40:46.209
I'm just going to say this
exactly as it's coming, how do

00:40:46.209 --> 00:40:51.246
you keep that power from corrupting you?
&gt;&gt; I have no idea,

00:40:51.246 --> 00:40:55.196
that's the honest answer, I have no idea.

00:40:58.384 --> 00:41:02.893
I've become increasingly isolated,
it's harder and harder for

00:41:02.893 --> 00:41:05.880
me to connect because I'm so
in my own mind.

00:41:06.930 --> 00:41:09.680
Like, how does this all play out?

00:41:09.680 --> 00:41:11.700
How do I fundraise?

00:41:11.700 --> 00:41:15.530
How do I help facilitate good investing
decisions which really means,

00:41:15.530 --> 00:41:20.160
how do I help this really great think
about a more platformized approach.

00:41:20.160 --> 00:41:26.260
And in that, I just get really Isolated
and so I don't have a good answer.

00:41:28.740 --> 00:41:34.370
I like things like this and I like to say
the things that I do and maybe it helps

00:41:34.370 --> 00:41:39.170
you in your own lives, but to be honest,
it's very selfish, it helps me because,

00:41:39.170 --> 00:41:42.649
like it reminds me, when I'm in a place
like, it reminds me of these stories.

00:41:43.980 --> 00:41:48.370
Just reminding myself like I made
$4.55 an hour at Burger King,

00:41:48.370 --> 00:41:52.110
I would get a $50 check and
I'd buy bus passes.

00:41:52.110 --> 00:41:56.280
That's awesome, I just,
I don't ever want to forget that and

00:41:56.280 --> 00:41:59.540
I'll go months and not remember that, but

00:41:59.540 --> 00:42:04.730
now as I say it, I'm like okay,
I started as a good earnest person.

00:42:04.730 --> 00:42:10.000
And I need to stay a good earnest person,
so I don't have a good answer.

00:42:10.000 --> 00:42:13.440
And I'll tell you of those 150 people,
maybe it's a little bit more,

00:42:13.440 --> 00:42:17.360
but I've met a lot of them,
it's really hard.

00:42:18.570 --> 00:42:24.450
It's very easy to end up
really drunk with power,

00:42:25.680 --> 00:42:30.220
it's really hard.
&gt;&gt; I really

00:42:30.220 --> 00:42:33.700
appreciate that candor because I don't
think that we get it very often.

00:42:34.760 --> 00:42:38.880
And you've laid out
an incredible vision for

00:42:38.880 --> 00:42:42.840
money as an instrument of change, so I'm
really grateful that you are here to share

00:42:42.840 --> 00:42:44.950
it with us today.
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:42:44.950 --> 00:42:46.967
&gt;&gt; We're going to open it up now to

00:42:46.967 --> 00:42:52.165
questions, I'm sure that there are many
followups on what you've explored,

00:42:52.165 --> 00:42:54.812
but really-.
&gt;&gt; Any feedback?

00:42:54.812 --> 00:42:57.348
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:42:57.348 --> 00:43:05.773
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:43:05.773 --> 00:43:10.355
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Hi,

00:43:10.355 --> 00:43:12.850
I'm Dana, I'm second year MBA at the GSB.

00:43:13.900 --> 00:43:16.029
Thank you for being with us today.

00:43:16.029 --> 00:43:20.110
But I'm not here to give feedback,
but I have a question for you.

00:43:20.110 --> 00:43:23.751
You just took Social Capital
