00:43:30.310 --> 00:43:34.712
&gt;&gt; Or it's just another

00:43:34.712 --> 00:43:39.536
way to get money
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:43:39.536 --> 00:43:41.588
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:43:41.588 --> 00:43:44.023
&gt;&gt; So awesome, yes.

00:43:44.023 --> 00:43:46.460
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:43:46.460 --> 00:43:47.369
&gt;&gt; But, no.

00:43:49.531 --> 00:43:55.570
So one of the things that I think about
a lot is, if I'm in a position to reshape.

00:43:55.570 --> 00:43:58.200
I've been in a position where
I was a part of a group

00:43:58.200 --> 00:44:00.450
that helped shape psychology of society.

00:44:00.450 --> 00:44:04.310
That's been a great experience and
I learned a lot.

00:44:04.310 --> 00:44:06.660
And so now in this position
what I say to myself is,

00:44:06.660 --> 00:44:09.510
what are the psychological
things I'd love to change now?

00:44:09.510 --> 00:44:12.300
And one of them is around
this idea of patience.

00:44:12.300 --> 00:44:15.250
And what's counterintuitive and I said
this earlier when we were just talking,

00:44:15.250 --> 00:44:21.250
is that, I think bringing
liquidity into companies sooner

00:44:21.250 --> 00:44:26.360
will actually allow companies to
bake longer and more patiently.

00:44:26.360 --> 00:44:29.510
And so part of what this vehicle
is is about bringing liquidity

00:44:29.510 --> 00:44:31.660
sooner in a company's lifecycle.

00:44:31.660 --> 00:44:35.180
because if we can take this company,
use it to acquire something else and

00:44:35.180 --> 00:44:38.160
that CEO is now able to
compensate their employees.

00:44:39.280 --> 00:44:40.710
As counterintuitive as it sounds,

00:44:40.710 --> 00:44:42.620
I suspect that they
are less likely to leave.

00:44:44.440 --> 00:44:47.451
And so now you could have employees stay
at a business for seven, eight, nine,

00:44:47.451 --> 00:44:52.780
ten years, which used to be
the historical norm and now it's not.

00:44:52.780 --> 00:44:57.030
Most of you,
probably are thinking to yourselves,

00:44:57.030 --> 00:45:00.740
well I'm going to get
a great job after the GSB.

00:45:00.740 --> 00:45:05.405
But I should probably portfolio manage my
career for the next six or seven years.

00:45:05.405 --> 00:45:08.769
18 months here, 18 months there,
18 months there, and

00:45:08.769 --> 00:45:11.010
then I'll decide what I really want to do.

00:45:11.010 --> 00:45:14.450
And part of it is because
there's an incentive to sort of

00:45:14.450 --> 00:45:17.070
build this kind of portfolio approach.

00:45:17.070 --> 00:45:20.490
But I suspect if you knew that
liquidity was a more traditional,

00:45:20.490 --> 00:45:23.910
predictable thing, many people
would actually say, you know what?

00:45:23.910 --> 00:45:26.400
I like these people here,
I like the mission of the company,

00:45:26.400 --> 00:45:29.150
I want to continue to
help advance their goals.

00:45:29.150 --> 00:45:32.500
And now that you've actually
solved my compensation problem,

00:45:32.500 --> 00:45:33.670
I'm willing to commit.

00:45:33.670 --> 00:45:37.690
So part of this vehicle
is trying to turn back

00:45:38.690 --> 00:45:42.630
the incentives towards people staying at
companies longer which I think is going to

00:45:42.630 --> 00:45:45.070
be required if you're going to
try to solve hard problems.

00:45:45.070 --> 00:45:49.242
because right now, the Silicon Valley
attrition rate in most companies is 20%.

00:45:49.242 --> 00:45:52.150
That means every five years,
your entire employee base is turned over.

00:45:52.150 --> 00:45:54.390
Think about that,
how do you do anything valuable,

00:45:54.390 --> 00:45:57.630
if everybody is new every five years?

00:45:57.630 --> 00:45:58.930
How is that possible?

00:45:58.930 --> 00:46:00.775
It's probably not possible.

00:46:00.775 --> 00:46:05.151
So that's what I would try to change and

00:46:05.151 --> 00:46:09.350
get the money.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:46:09.350 --> 00:46:11.980
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Carolyn, I'm also an MBA too.

00:46:11.980 --> 00:46:15.840
Your sports analogy of kind of buckling
down and going into hyperdrive really

00:46:15.840 --> 00:46:19.330
resonated with me,
I also realize that that can go too far.

00:46:19.330 --> 00:46:22.560
So I'm curious to hear how you make some
of the tough calls on whether it's not to

00:46:22.560 --> 00:46:24.820
participate in the next
round of fundraising or

00:46:24.820 --> 00:46:25.910
whatever that looks like to you.

00:46:25.910 --> 00:46:27.129
How do you make those decisions?

00:46:30.456 --> 00:46:33.389
This is, honestly, this is, so yesterday,

00:46:33.389 --> 00:46:37.770
we spent an inordinate amount
of time talking about this.

00:46:37.770 --> 00:46:41.570
We've made a bunch of decisions
that I deeply regret,

00:46:41.570 --> 00:46:44.430
because they were entirely
monetary in nature.

00:46:44.430 --> 00:46:49.329
And what I said to the team was, I'm fine
with us making purely monetary decisions,

00:46:49.329 --> 00:46:51.340
but we didn't even do those well.

00:46:51.340 --> 00:46:54.674
It's not as if we had a predictable
rate of return on that that said,

00:46:54.674 --> 00:46:55.660
yeah you now what?

00:46:55.660 --> 00:47:00.520
We're just going to close our eyes here,
double our money, and walk away.

00:47:00.520 --> 00:47:03.390
And so,
the reason I brought that up to them,

00:47:03.390 --> 00:47:09.530
was I was like our decision-making
can't bleed over time.

00:47:09.530 --> 00:47:15.060
And right now, it's very hard for
us as an organization to know

00:47:15.060 --> 00:47:17.770
how much should be mission driven,
how much should not.

00:47:17.770 --> 00:47:20.380
When should we really double down
on things that are not working,

00:47:20.380 --> 00:47:20.970
when should we not.

00:47:20.970 --> 00:47:24.280
When should we just give up,
because its just either the timing is

00:47:24.280 --> 00:47:29.556
not the right time and quite honestly
I don’t have a very good answer.

00:47:29.556 --> 00:47:34.850
But if it is the thing
that we are now the most

00:47:34.850 --> 00:47:40.330
emotionally sensitive to, because for
example the opposite may be true.

00:47:40.330 --> 00:47:44.328
So if the diabetes business turns out to
work, does the hundred and first person

00:47:44.328 --> 00:47:47.960
that works at Social Capital say fuck it,
we're just going to buckle down and when

00:47:47.960 --> 00:47:51.050
they're writing checks all of a sudden
we're 50 million deep into something that

00:47:51.050 --> 00:47:53.820
is clearly not going to work was that a?
&gt;&gt; So,

00:47:53.820 --> 00:47:57.720
I don't have a really good answer but it
is the thing that I think about the most

00:47:57.720 --> 00:48:01.640
when do you know how do you
know to keep going for?

00:48:01.640 --> 00:48:03.240
When do you know that it's just too much?

00:48:03.240 --> 00:48:06.090
When do you know that your decision
making is bleeding into a vein that

00:48:06.090 --> 00:48:07.020
is not constructive?

00:48:08.450 --> 00:48:09.080
I have no idea.

00:48:11.250 --> 00:48:14.042
So it's a great question,
I think about that a lot.

00:48:16.504 --> 00:48:21.040
By the way, a lot of things, it's really
powerful to be able to say I don't know.

00:48:21.040 --> 00:48:24.630
I mean, I would just encourage you guys
all to just be like, I don't know.

00:48:24.630 --> 00:48:29.970
This culture, American culture is
this weird thing of know-it-alls.

00:48:29.970 --> 00:48:31.850
Everybody doesn't know everything.

00:48:31.850 --> 00:48:35.050
How he fuck can you ever learn?

00:48:35.050 --> 00:48:36.310
When is that going to be valuable?

00:48:37.930 --> 00:48:42.570
Don't be the, just own that one line,
I don't know.

00:48:42.570 --> 00:48:45.000
It's so powerful.

00:48:45.000 --> 00:48:49.110
And as I kind of get more abstracted,
when I meet people, and

00:48:49.110 --> 00:48:54.030
I ask them things, it is is so
endearing to hear, I don't know.

00:48:54.030 --> 00:48:57.496
Because behind that is just
the self-awareness and

00:48:57.496 --> 00:49:00.430
confidence that I think
is increasingly rare.

00:49:02.300 --> 00:49:05.695
Now you guys are like, I'm going to
fucking grin fuck this guy and

00:49:05.695 --> 00:49:07.140
just keep telling everybody, I don't know.

00:49:07.140 --> 00:49:07.969
Don't do that.

00:49:07.969 --> 00:49:08.868
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I mean really own it.

00:49:08.868 --> 00:49:11.532
If you're going to say it,
really own it, but

00:49:11.532 --> 00:49:16.070
I really think it's a very powerful
thing to be able to say, I don't know.

00:49:18.875 --> 00:49:22.260
Hi, my name is and
I'm a first year MBA student.

00:49:22.260 --> 00:49:26.580
I'm also a Canadian who went to Waterloo
and my first job was at Burger King, so

00:49:26.580 --> 00:49:28.430
I really see you as a role model.
&gt;&gt; You're like my fucking

00:49:28.430 --> 00:49:29.090
brother.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:49:29.090 --> 00:49:30.720
&gt;&gt; I mean no literally,

00:49:30.720 --> 00:49:32.290
you look like me too, it's crazy.

00:49:32.290 --> 00:49:33.335
It's the weirdest thing.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:49:33.335 --> 00:49:34.130
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:49:34.130 --> 00:49:35.710
&gt;&gt; Are you planted, are you an actor?

00:49:35.710 --> 00:49:36.760
&gt;&gt; I swear,

00:49:36.760 --> 00:49:38.525
I swear to God.
&gt;&gt; Well,

00:49:38.525 --> 00:49:42.260
you're really freaking me out right now.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:49:42.260 --> 00:49:43.265
&gt;&gt; What do you want?

00:49:43.265 --> 00:49:46.870
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:49:46.870 --> 00:49:48.600
&gt;&gt; So my question for you is,

00:49:48.600 --> 00:49:51.193
a of couple years ago at an event at HBS,

00:49:51.193 --> 00:49:55.616
you mentioned that MBA students
are typically at a disadvantage.

00:49:55.616 --> 00:49:59.021
With respect to getting capital if they
pursue entrepreneurship Do you think that

00:49:59.021 --> 00:50:00.070
still holds true today?

00:50:00.070 --> 00:50:03.887
And if so [CROSSTALK].
&gt;&gt; So, that's a perfect example of me,

00:50:03.887 --> 00:50:08.543
before Cas, which Dean Leven talked about,
